  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Addressing deforestation and livelihoods through Inclusive Coffee Supply Chains</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Indonesia ranked fourth in the world as a coffee grower and exporter, with Sumatra Island accounting for more than half of the producing areas. Most coffee farmers barely earn the sufficient income required to have a decent standard of living for all family members. As a result, coffee production is tied to deforestation: having low productivity, many coffee farmers resort to turning rainforests into cropland, taking advantage of soil fertility on those areas. With coffee production in Indonesia anticipated to decline due to climate change, these forests are at risk of being deforested even more to expand coffee farms.The project goal is to advocate responsible production of coffee in areas that are critical to biodiversity, while also promoting gender equality across responsible business practices. This is to be achieved by building capacities for coffee actors (government, companies, farmers) to reduce deforestation and protect biodiversity, promoting an inclusive and gender equal coffee supply chain aimed at improving livelihoods, and strengthening resilience against climate change impacts among coffee farming families. The project will build narratives that provide holistic multidimensional root causes for deforestation and show how a more sustainable model of landscape and community projects has the power to reduce deforestation.Human Rights and Environmental Due Diligence (HREDD), is a process by which companies identify, prevent, mitigate, and account for human rights and environmental risks and impacts in their supply chains, which is a fundamental corporate responsibility. Both the UN Guiding Principles and the OECD Due Diligence Guidance stress the need for due diligence to be risk-based, for companies to identify, prevent, mitigate and account for how they address adverse impacts in their operations and supply chains, prioritising the most salient issues. Recent EU regulation (and regulation from some countries such as Germany) has brought to the fore the need for companies to comply with such practices. A central aspect of this project will be to support the partners in developing and implementing an approach to identify and mitigate some of the risks and challenges in their Indonesia coffee supply chain. This will be demonstrated by the development of a set of recommendations and measures to mitigate and remediate CSR impacts and risks through improved company policies and processes identified.</narrative>
  </description>
